# modules.cpp colour LCD controller

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.1 specifications. This document specifies the
implemented colour LCD controller module and its ST7789 driver. mm.lcd, the
ST7789 controller, the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board provider, and the
display-demo application exist. Visual panel behavior on hardware remains a
hardware-qualification requirement rather than a completed claim.

docs/modules-display.mdy owns the portable display boundary and platform
binding. docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns the implemented MCU provider
model. docs/modules-platform-pico.mdy owns the Pico SDK bridge. This document
specifies the colour controller beneath mm.display and must not weaken any of
those boundaries.

# Separation of responsibilities

Four layers own four different facts:

- mm.mcu owns reusable SPI and GPIO operations.
- A controller module owns the controller command protocol and refresh state.
- A display provider owns panel parameters and fixed physical wiring.
- A board profile states that the provider's display is actually attached.

The Pico SDK adapter does not expose LCD commands. The controller does not
include Pico SDK headers. The portable application does not know which
controller or GPIOs were selected.

# Relation to mm.display and mm.epaper

mm.display is the portable boundary. It defines geometry, initialization,
packed pixel writes, clear, refresh, and sleep. ePaper controllers implement
that boundary for one-bit and tri-colour panels where refresh is a physical
operation that rewrites the panel. LCD controllers implement the same boundary
for sixteen-bit colour panels where refresh has nothing left to do: the panel
shows what was written the moment it was written.

The difference that reaches mm.display is the pixel format, not the lifecycle:
these panels report sixteen bits per pixel and answer `Ok` to refresh because
the visible state is already the controller's frame memory. An ePaper panel
reports one bit per pixel and refresh drives a physical waveform. A portable
application writes a rectangle and calls refresh; the answer differs by
controller but the call is the same.

# Portable interface

The controller implements the existing mm.display::Display interface. It does
not add a new virtual function, a new colour enum, or a new pixel format
beyond what mm.display already defines.

The controller reports:

Opening fence
Geometry geometry() const override;
// width, height from the panel descriptor; bits_per_pixel is 16
Closing fence

`write` takes an mm.display::Rectangle and a byte span of RGB565 pixels,
most significant byte first. It sets the window in the controller and streams
the pixels. `refresh` answers Ok and sends nothing. `clear` maps the three
mm.display::Color values onto their RGB565 encodings and fills the named
rectangle. `sleep` puts the controller in its low-power state and the display
off.

The three named colours are what a portable smoke test can ask of any panel.
A caller that wants one of the other sixty-five thousand composes RGB565
pixels and writes them.

# Controller driver

mm.lcd.st7789 implements the Sitronix ST7789 colour TFT controller over
portable mm.mcu SPI and GPIO. It contains no vendor header and no board pin
number: a board's provider supplies the wiring, the panel timings, and the
initialisation sequence, and this module drives the protocol.

The driver receives immutable wiring and panel descriptors:

Opening fence
struct Wiring {
    mm::mcu::SpiConfiguration spi;
    unsigned int chip_select_gpio;
    unsigned int data_command_gpio;
    unsigned int reset_gpio;
    std::optional<unsigned int> backlight_gpio;
    unsigned long reset_step_ms;
    unsigned long sleep_out_ms;
};

struct InitializationCommand {
    std::byte command;
    std::span<const std::byte> data;
};

struct Panel {
    unsigned int width;
    unsigned int height;
    std::span<const InitializationCommand> initialization;
    std::byte memory_access;
    bool inverted;
    unsigned int column_offset;
    unsigned int row_offset;
};
Closing fence

Command data is provider-owned static storage and must outlive the Controller.
Porch, power, and gamma settings differ between panels carrying the same
controller, so the board supplies them rather than the module guessing. The
memory access control byte and inversion flag are board facts: the module only
sends them. The visible area may sit at an offset inside the controller's
frame memory, which is what makes a 240-wide panel on a 240-wide controller
still need a column offset on some boards.

The driver owns a state machine distinguishing Idle, Ready, and Sleeping.
Initialization configures SPI, performs the documented reset sequence (hold
low, release, wait), sends the provider's initialisation commands, sets the
pixel format to sixteen bits, applies memory access control and inversion, and
turns the display on. A backlight GPIO, when present, is raised at initialize.

Reset uses the reference driver's high-low-high sequence with configurable
step delays. Sleep-out wait is a fixed delay after the 0x11 command.

Two things in the Waveshare LCD_2in8 reference driver are deliberately not
reproduced. Its scan direction is absent, because mm.display writes
rectangles and rotation belongs to the caller for the same reason it does in
mm.touch. Its full-framebuffer Display path is absent: mm.display writes
bounded rectangles and does not require a whole-screen framebuffer.

# Provider binding

The controller is bound through the existing mm.display platform-interface
mechanism. A concrete hardware board binds mm.display to a provider that
constructs the ST7789 controller:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.display platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.display
Closing fence

The provider imports mm.display, mm.lcd.st7789, and mm.mcu. It owns the fixed
SPI wiring, pin assignments, and panel descriptor, constructs the controller,
and registers the Display implementation from a retained static object.
Existing provider validation, board-over-SDK precedence, conditional closure
injection, and core platform-edge rules apply unchanged.

An SDK must not provide a default LCD binding merely because it can drive
one. The SDK supplies buses; attachment is a board-profile fact.

# Test strategy

Controller tests use a fake MCU Platform that records SPI and GPIO operations.
Tests compare semantic command transcripts rather than merely checking that a
source file compiled.

Required host tests cover:

- SPI configuration and transfer ordering;
- reset sequence and step delays;
- command versus data GPIO transitions;
- initialization transcript including pixel format and memory access;
- window setting and RGB565 pixel streaming;
- rectangle and data-length validation;
- clear maps the three named colours to their RGB565 values;
- refresh answers Ok without sending a command;
- sleep and the reinitialization path;
- transport errors propagated without continuing the command sequence;
- no controller or provider objects in an unrelated executable closure.

Target qualification builds one portable app through the real Pico external
CMake bridge, validates the ELF and UF2, and confirms exactly one display and
MCU provider registration object. It then flashes through flash.sh.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add mm.mcu SPI facility described by
   docs/modules-epaper.mdy.
2. Implemented: add mm.lcd.st7789 with initialization, write, clear, and
   sleep tests against fake SPI and GPIO.
3. Implemented: add the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 display provider binding
   mm.display to the ST7789 controller.
4. Implemented: add apps/display-demo drawing three RGB565 colour bars.
5. Pending hardware: qualify initialization, colour bars, rotation, clear,
   and sleep on the named Waveshare board.

# Acceptance criteria

- An app importing only mm.display links with exactly one selected display
  provider and the provider's controller closure.
- The same app is unavailable on a board with no display binding.
- An app not importing mm.display contains no display provider or controller
  registration object.
- Controller host tests pass without Pico SDK or physical hardware.
- SPI payloads cross the platform boundary as spans, not byte-at-a-time
  virtual calls.
- No controller source includes Pico SDK headers.
- A successful link or flash is not reported as a successful screen refresh.
- The display-demo red bar draws red (0xf800), not blue, catching a
  byte-swap in the RGB565 encoding.

# What this does not settle

- ST7789 is the first implemented LCD controller. Its scan-direction
  variants, other pixel formats, and DMA-driven SPI are not implemented.
- Arbitrary image composition, fonts, drawing primitives, and UI layout are
  outside this module and belong in layers above mm.display.
- Backlight PWM dimming is not implemented; a panel with a plain on-off
  backlight line is raised at initialize and left.
- Multi-display selection and hot-plug discovery are not specified.
- Grayscale and bit depths other than sixteen are not promised.
